1. A method of evaluating defects on a polished semiconductor wafer, the method comprising:

directing, by an incident optical system of a laser surface-inspection device, a laser beam onto a surface of the polished semiconductor wafer at a predetermined incident angle while relatively scanning the laser beam and the polished semiconductor wafer;

receiving, during the scanning, light generated from the surface in response to irradiation by the laser beam with three different light-receiving systems, the three different light-receiving systems comprising:  
(a) a first light-receiving system configured to receive scattered light in a first angular range,  
(b) a second light-receiving system configured to receive scattered light in a second angular range different from the first angular range, and  
(c) a third light-receiving system configured to receive reflected light or scattered light in a third angular range different from the first and second angular ranges;

generating, from outputs of the first, second, and third light-receiving systems, respective detection signals corresponding to respective positions on the polished semiconductor wafer;

identifying defect candidates on the polished semiconductor wafer based on at least one of the respective detection signals;

comparing signal characteristics of the defect candidates among the outputs of the first, second, and third light-receiving systems; and

classifying or evaluating the defect candidates as at least one type of wafer-surface defect based on a relationship among the signal characteristics obtained from the three different light-receiving systems.

1. A method for detecting light point defects on a semiconductor wafer, the method comprising:

irradiating a surface of the semiconductor wafer with inspection light;

receiving light from the surface of the semiconductor wafer by a first light-receiving system configured to generate first measurement results;

receiving light from the surface of the semiconductor wafer by a second light-receiving system configured to generate second measurement results, the second light-receiving system having a light-receiving condition different from that of the first light-receiving system;

receiving light from the surface of the semiconductor wafer by a third light-receiving system configured to generate third measurement results, the third light-receiving system having a light-receiving condition different from those of the first and second light-receiving systems;

identifying candidate light point defects on the semiconductor wafer based on at least one of the first measurement results, the second measurement results, or the third measurement results;

comparing, for each candidate light point defect, the first measurement results, the second measurement results, and the third measurement results corresponding to a location of the candidate light point defect; and

classifying the candidate light point defect as at least one of a process-induced defect or adhered foreign matter based on a relationship among the first measurement results, the second measurement results, and the third measurement results.

A method for evaluating a semiconductor wafer, the method comprising:

irradiating a surface of the semiconductor wafer with laser light by means of an incident optical system of a laser surface inspection apparatus;

receiving, by means of a light-receiving optical system of the laser surface inspection apparatus, light scattered from the surface of the semiconductor wafer as a result of the irradiation with the laser light;

detecting, based on an output signal from the light-receiving optical system, abnormalities and foreign particles present on the surface of the semiconductor wafer; and

evaluating the semiconductor wafer based on detection results obtained by the laser surface inspection apparatus.

In the method, the incident optical system is configured to direct the laser light onto the wafer surface at a predetermined incident angle, and the light-receiving optical system is configured to receive scattered light generated from surface defects, surface irregularities, or foreign matter adhering to the wafer surface. The presence, position, size, or number of the abnormalities and foreign particles is determined from the intensity or distribution of the received scattered light, thereby enabling evaluation of the surface quality of the semiconductor wafer.
